      "notes": "Ask pupils to answer independently on mini-whiteboards, then compare explanations. Check that they distinguish particle movement from the movement of the whole object. Reteach if pupils say particles in solids do not move or that compressed particles become smaller.\nAnswers: 1. Liquid particles can move past one another, so water takes the bowl’s shape. The particles stay close together, so the same amount of water keeps the same volume. 2. Air compresses more easily because its particles are far apart, leaving gaps that can become smaller. Solid particles are already close together and vibrate in fixed positions."
