      "notes": "Follow the chain from left to right. For freezing, explain that particles transfer energy to the surroundings, move less freely and become arranged in fixed positions, where they still vibrate. For melting, energy allows particles to leave their fixed positions; do not imply that temperature must rise during melting. For evaporation, explain that particles escape at the liquid’s surface; it can happen without boiling. In condensation, particles lose energy, slow down and become close together, but can still move past one another. Ask pupils to identify the two changes that transfer energy into the substance and the two that transfer energy out.\nCondensation: energy leaves a gas. Particles slow down and come close together, forming a liquid. Particles do not change size."
