      "notes": "Collect individual answers before sharing the models. Look for correct energy-transfer directions, movement rather than changes in particle size, and close spacing in both solids and liquids. Use errors to plan retrieval and reteaching.\nAnswers: 1. During melting, energy transfers into the ice. Particles leave their fixed positions and move past one another while remaining close together. During freezing, energy transfers out of the water. Particles become arranged in fixed positions and vibrate there. 2. During evaporation, energy transfers into the liquid and some faster particles escape from its surface. In the gas, particles are far apart and move freely. During condensation, energy transfers out of the gas. Particles slow down and come close together to form liquid droplets. 3. Water vapour has large gaps between its particles that can be reduced. Liquid water’s particles are already close together, so it is difficult to compress."
